"""Chapter 2: Literature Survey (Mosaic, CSDS-A-A16)."""

from . import facts as F
from . import lit
from .blocks import B, EQ, FIG, H1, H2, H3, N, P, TAB
from .refs import CITE as R


def blocks():
    D = F.DATA
    return [
        H1("2. LITERATURE SURVEY"),
        H2("2.1 Overview"),
        P("This chapter surveys the work behind the project in five steps. It first analyses the base paper in detail, because the pipeline of Mosaic is a re-implementation of its idea; it then groups the related "
          "work by theme (compound-expression theory and datasets, deep facial expression recognition, GAN-based recognition, compound-expression recognition, and generative-model evaluation); it describes the datasets and "
          "algorithms that appear in that work; it compares the strengths and limitations of the approaches; and it closes with the research gap that the project addresses."),
        P("Sources fall into three groups according to how much of each could be read. The base paper, the three papers kept in the batch folder (Galkaduwa, Liu et al. and Su and Rosdi) and the standard references were "
          "read or checked against their records; four items of the batch survey (references "
          f"[{R['cdgan']}] to [{R['facecycle']}]) were verified only through their bibliographic records and, for two of them, the abstract. For those items this chapter states only what the record or abstract says "
          "and never quotes a result."),
        H2("2.2 Base Paper Analysis"),
        P(f"The base paper is \"{lit.BASE_PAPER_TITLE}\" by Khine, Siritanawan and Kotani, published in IEEE Access, volume 13, 2025 [{R['base']}]. Its aim is to estimate complex emotions, defined as mixtures of two or more "
          "basic emotions, from a still face image."),
        H3("2.2.1 Method"),
        P("The approach has two parts, shown in the paper as *analysis by synthesis*."),
        N("**Generation (cEmoGANs).** A conditional GAN generator takes a latent vector z and a six-dimensional multi-hot label (anger, disgust, fear, happiness, sadness, surprise; a dimension is 1 when the emotion is present) and produces a face. "
          "The discriminator receives the same label. An encoder with two convolution layers, trained to minimise the mean absolute error between an estimated latent and the one predicted from an image, projects a real face into the "
          "latent space so that the identity can be held fixed while the label changes the expression. The discriminator learned faster than the generator in preliminary experiments, so learning rates of 1e-5 (discriminator) and 1e-4 (generator) were used with Adam.",
          "**Recognition.** A small convolutional network (convolution layers, a max-pooling layer, flattening and a sigmoid output layer) is trained on the generated faces with a cross-entropy loss applied to each sigmoid output independently, "
          "so that each emotion is detected on its own and several can be active together."),
        P("The conditional-GAN training objective is the usual one, with the label y given to both networks:"),
        EQ("L_cGAN = E_x[ log D(x | y) ] + E_z[ log( 1 − D( G(z | y) | y ) ) ]"),
        P("The paper prints the second term without the \"1 −\"; the standard form is shown here. Because a single sigmoid output per emotion cannot be judged by accuracy alone, the paper evaluates recognition with the f-beta score "
          "(computed one emotion against the rest) and with the exact match ratio (EMR), the share of images whose whole predicted label set equals the true set."),
        H3("2.2.2 Data and experimental setting"),
        P(f"Experiments use {F.BP['datasets']}. Because these datasets are posed, laboratory collections, the authors select peak-expression images (for the MUG image sequences) and morph two expressions together to obtain ground-truth "
          f"mixtures. The reported hardware is {F.BP['hardware']}. For the recognition experiments, 15,000 images generated by cEmoGANs (and, for comparison, by StyleGANs) are split 7:3 into training and test sets."),
        H3("2.2.3 Reported results"),
        TAB("Results reported in the base paper (read from its Tables 2 to 5 and Section IV)",
            ["Evaluation", "CK+", "JAFFE", "MUG"],
            [["Inception score of generated faces (15-class expression model; higher is better)", "8.8", "9.0", "11.4 (best of all models)"],
             ["Fréchet distance of generated faces (lower is better)", "56.4", "107.8", "136.6"],
             ["BRISQUE distortion score (lower is better)", "20.1", "31.8", "11.0 (best of all models)"],
             ["Identity kept (cosine similarity above 0.7 between a generated face and its re-encoded version)", "98 %", "94 %", "99 %"],
             ["f-beta of the multi-label recogniser trained on cEmoGAN faces", "0.99", "0.99", "0.80"],
             ["Exact match ratio of the same recogniser", "100 %", "99 %", "68.7 %"],
             ["f-beta of an EfficientNet-B0 trained on the same data", "0.45", "0.60", "0.51"],
             ["f-beta of the same CNN trained on StyleGAN faces", "0.42", "0.54", "0.53"]],
            widths=[3.6, 0.8, 0.8, 1.4], align=["l", "c", "c", "c"]),
        P("The paper also checks that the generator really mixes expressions, by counting the facial action units that fire in generated faces and comparing them with the action units of the morphed ground truth, and checks "
          "feature disentanglement by swapping the label vectors of two latent samples and observing that the expressions swap. For unconstrained photographs (an award ceremony, an Olympic win, a roller coaster) it reports only a qualitative "
          "comparison with the Py-Feat recogniser, and it notes that its recogniser, compared with EfficientNet-B0 on basic emotions, misreads sadness as anger and fear as happiness on CK+."),
        H3("2.2.4 Strengths, weaknesses and what this project takes from it"),
        B("**Strength:** the idea of generating the scarce class (mixtures) instead of collecting it, and of formulating the recogniser as multi-label so that exactly the compound outputs are available.",
          "**Strength:** a broad set of generator checks (action units, identity cosine similarity, inception score, Fréchet distance, BRISQUE) rather than a single number.",
          "**Weakness:** every recognition score in Table 5 is measured on images produced by the same generator that trained the model, on posed laboratory data, so it does not show performance on real photographs; the authors acknowledge possible demographic bias.",
          "**Weakness:** the work depends on a GPU workstation and a morphing step to obtain ground truth that most projects do not have."),
        P("Mosaic adopts the two-stage idea (condition a generator on two emotions, train a sigmoid-output network on the result) and the multi-label formulation. It does not implement the identity encoder, and it evaluates on real "
          "FER2013 faces rather than on generated ones; Chapter 8 therefore does not rank Mosaic against Table 5 but sets the figures side by side and states plainly that the conditions differ."),
        H2("2.3 Related Work"),
        H3("2.3.1 Compound expressions: theory and datasets"),
        P(f"Du, Tao and Martinez [{R['du2014']}] established, from images of people producing them on request, that compound facial expressions of emotion are consistently produced and recognised, and defined categories such as happily surprised "
          f"and sadly angry. The eleven compound labels of Mosaic (happily surprised, happily disgusted, sadly fearful, sadly angry, sadly surprised, sadly disgusted, fearfully angry, fearfully surprised, angrily surprised, angrily disgusted "
          f"and disgustedly surprised) are pairs of basic emotions in that spirit. A real-world source of compound labels is RAF-DB [{R['rafdb']}], whose crowd-sourced annotation includes compound categories; it is the "
          f"data on which the ABAW7 solution discussed below was pre-trained [{R['abaw7']}]. It is distributed on request and was not available to this project."),
        H3("2.3.2 Deep facial expression recognition and baselines"),
        P(f"The survey of Li and Deng [{R['lideng']}] describes the shift from hand-crafted features to deep networks and the datasets used. A recent controlled comparison by Galkaduwa [{R['galkaduwa']}] trains HOG with an SVM, "
          f"local binary patterns with logistic regression and a lightweight three-block CNN on FER-2013, CK+ and KDEF. On FER-2013 it reports accuracies of 44.0 % (HOG+SVM), 25.0 % (LBP) and 51.6 % (CNN), while on the laboratory dataset CK+ the "
          f"numbers are 98.4 %, 25.0 % and 96.9 %; the paper concludes that dataset complexity matters more than the choice of feature representation. This is the context for the {F.BASE['accuracy'] * 100:.1f} % of Mosaic's single-label "
          f"backbone and {F.FIN['accuracy'] * 100:.1f} % of its final network: FER2013 is a hard, noisy dataset on which small CNNs score around half of the images. Su and Rosdi [{R['su']}] review 62 multimodal emotion-recognition studies "
          "published between 2020 and 2025 and report that CNNs remain the main choice for visual features, while models are computationally demanding and generalise poorly across settings."),
        H3("2.3.3 GAN-based expression recognition"),
        P(f"Several recent papers use GANs to improve recognition rather than to create mixtures. Liu, Ni and Cai [{R['cdgan']}] propose a contrastive disentangled GAN (CD-GAN) that uses separate encoders for identity and expression, "
          "combined with contrastive learning, because expression features overlap strongly with identity features; the abstract reports results comparable to state of the art on laboratory and in-the-wild datasets. "
          f"Khemakhem and Ltifi [{R['nstgan']}] propose NST-GAN, which uses neural style transfer to remove identity cues and keep expression, and test it on CK+, FER-2013 and JAFFE. Two further batch-survey items, a multi-level integration "
          f"disentangled GAN [{R['migan']}] and a face-cycle GAN for cross-domain recognition [{R['facecycle']}], are cited here for completeness; only their titles and venues were verified. All of these assign one basic label to a face."),
        P(f"General-purpose conditional generators are also relevant: StarGAN [{R['stargan']}] translates a face between several attribute domains with a single network, and GANimation [{R['ganimation']}] "
          "animates a face from a single image under action-unit control. They show that expression can be edited with a condition vector, which is the mechanism cEmoGANs use."),
        H3("2.3.4 Compound-expression recognition"),
        P(f"Liu et al. [{R['abaw7']}] address the ABAW7 compound-expression challenge with an ensemble of a ResNet-50, a Vision Transformer and PosterV2, pre-trained on RAF-DB and fused late. On the RAF-DB compound validation set (seven compound "
          "classes) their Table 1 gives an accuracy of 62.13 % and F1 of 54.23 % for the ViT, 64.34 % and 55.36 % for PosterV2 and 60.35 % and 51.54 % for the ResNet; the paper adds that the ensemble can recognise some compound "
          "expressions of the challenge videos through zero-shot learning. These numbers are the only compound-class scores on real faces in the surveyed material, and they were obtained with real compound labels, which Mosaic lacks."),
        H3("2.3.5 Generative models, their evaluation and multi-label learning"),
        P(f"The generative adversarial network of Goodfellow et al. [{R['gan']}] trains a generator and a discriminator against each other; the conditional version of Mirza and Osindero [{R['cgan']}] feeds a label to both, and "
          f"DCGAN [{R['dcgan']}] established convolutional architectures with batch normalisation as a stable design, which the Mosaic generator follows (transposed convolutions with batch normalisation and ReLU, tanh output). Quality is judged "
          f"with the inception score [{R['improvedgan']}] and the Fréchet inception distance [{R['fid']}], both of which the base paper uses with an expression network in place of Inception. For the recognition side, multi-label "
          f"learning [{R['multilabel']}] treats each label as its own binary decision, which is what a bank of sigmoid outputs trained with binary cross-entropy implements."),
        H2("2.4 Datasets and Algorithms Used in the Surveyed Work"),
        TAB("Datasets named in the surveyed work and in this project",
            ["Dataset", "Kind", "Labels", "Role"],
            [["FER2013 [" + str(R['fer2013']) + "]", "Unconstrained, 48x48 grayscale", "7 basic emotions", f"Used by Mosaic: {D['n_train']:,} training and {D['n_test']:,} test images"],
             ["CK+ [" + str(R['ckplus']) + "]", "Laboratory, posed", "Basic emotions", "Base paper; Galkaduwa (constrained, low difficulty)"],
             ["JAFFE", "Laboratory, Japanese female subjects", "Basic emotions", "Base paper; image morphing for mixtures"],
             ["MUG", "Laboratory image sequences", "Basic emotions", "Base paper; peak frames, morphed mixtures"],
             ["KDEF", "Laboratory, medium difficulty", "Basic emotions", "Galkaduwa"],
             ["RAF-DB [" + str(R['rafdb']) + "]", "In the wild", "Basic and compound", "ABAW7 pre-training; on request, not available here"],
             ["AffectNet [" + str(R['affectnet']) + "]", "In the wild, large", "Categories, valence, arousal", "Cited for context"]],
            widths=[1.2, 1.7, 1.4, 2.0], align=["l", "l", "l", "l"]),
        FIG(F.fig("fig_class_distribution.png"), "Class distribution of the FER2013 data used by Mosaic. Disgust has only 436 training images, a point that matters again in Chapter 8.", 5.2),
        P("The algorithms that recur are convolutional networks (the base paper's small CNN, Galkaduwa's three-block CNN, the CNN branch of the ABAW7 ensemble), transformers (ViT, PosterV2), and conditional or disentangled GANs. "
          f"For face localisation, Mosaic uses the Viola-Jones boosted cascade of Haar-like features [{R['viola']}] as implemented in OpenCV [{R['opencv']}], trained with Adam [{R['adam']}], batch normalisation [{R['bn']}] and dropout [{R['dropout']}] on PyTorch [{R['pytorch']}]."),
        H2("2.5 Comparison, Strengths and Limitations"),
        TAB("Surveyed work: approach, finding and limitation", ["Work", "Year", "Approach", "Finding / limitation"], lit.LIT_TABLE, widths=[1.5, 0.5, 2.3, 2.3], size=9.5, align=["l", "c", "l", "l"]),
        P("Two patterns stand out. First, the recognition papers that use GANs (CD-GAN, NST-GAN, the face-cycle GAN) use them to clean identity out of the features and still give one basic label per face; only the base paper uses the generator to create "
          "mixtures. Second, the only compound scores on real faces (the ABAW7 solution) rely on real compound labels and heavy models, and the only high compound scores that rely on synthetic data (the base paper) are measured on the synthetic data itself."),
        TAB("Strengths and limitations of the approach families", ["Family", "Strength", "Limitation"],
            [["Hand-crafted features + classical classifier", "Cheap, interpretable, strong on controlled data", "Collapses on unconstrained data (FER-2013: 44.0 % and 25.0 %)"],
             ["Small CNN, single label", "Learns features end to end; runs on a CPU", "One label per face; about half the FER-2013 images right"],
             ["Disentangling / style-transfer GANs", "Separate identity from expression, improve robustness", "Still one basic label; heavier training"],
             ["Analysis by synthesis (base paper)", "Creates the scarce mixed-emotion data; multi-label output", "Evaluated on its own generated images; needs encoder, morphing and a GPU"],
             ["Ensembles of large models on real compound labels", "Real compound scores; transferable", "Needs a compound-labelled dataset; heavy"]],
            widths=[2.0, 2.2, 2.4], align=["l", "l", "l"]),
        H2("2.6 Research Gap"),
        P("The survey leaves the following gap, which is both the batch statement and what the reading shows."),
        B(*lit.GAPS),
        P("Mosaic positions itself in that gap as a transparent, runnable and tested re-implementation. It cannot close the gap on real compound recognition, because the data is missing; what it can do, and what Chapters 6 to 8 do, is to "
          "build the whole chain, switch the synthetic step on and off under controlled seeds, and report the generator's fidelity and the behaviour of the compound outputs as measured, so that the next iteration (Chapter 10) starts from facts."),
    ]
